Subtask 1.2: Run Baseline Monitoring While Generating Load
Rather than monitoring an idle system, this subtask captures a controlled monitoring window while a stress workload runs concurrently, so the collected data reflects real contention on CPU, memory, and disk.

Step 1: Create the monitoring script. It runs for a fixed duration and captures top, iostat, sar, vmstat, and a manual memory sampling loop, all writing into a timestamped directory.

cat > scripts/performance_monitor.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

TIMESTAMP=$(date +"%Y%m%d_%H%M%S")
MONITOR_DIR="/opt/performance-review/monitoring/${TIMESTAMP}"
DURATION=120   # seconds; kept short for lab time budget
SAMPLE_INTERVAL=2
SAMPLES=$((DURATION / SAMPLE_INTERVAL))

mkdir -p "$MONITOR_DIR"
cd "$MONITOR_DIR" || exit 1

echo "Starting monitoring run: $TIMESTAMP"
echo "Duration: ${DURATION}s, samples: ${SAMPLES}"

top -b -d "$SAMPLE_INTERVAL" -n "$SAMPLES" > top_output.txt &
TOP_PID=$!

iostat -x "$SAMPLE_INTERVAL" "$SAMPLES" > iostat_output.txt &
IOSTAT_PID=$!

sar -u -r -d "$SAMPLE_INTERVAL" "$SAMPLES" > sar_output.txt &
SAR_PID=$!

vmstat "$SAMPLE_INTERVAL" "$SAMPLES" > vmstat_output.txt &
VMSTAT_PID=$!

: > memory_tracking.txt
remaining=$DURATION
while [ "$remaining" -gt 0 ]; do
    echo "$(date +%s) $(free -m | awk '/Mem:/ {print $2, $3}')" >> memory_tracking.txt
    sleep "$SAMPLE_INTERVAL"
    remaining=$((remaining - SAMPLE_INTERVAL))
done &
MEM_PID=$!

echo "$TOP_PID $IOSTAT_PID $SAR_PID $VMSTAT_PID $MEM_PID" > monitor_pids.txt

wait $TOP_PID $IOSTAT_PID $SAR_PID $VMSTAT_PID $MEM_PID 2>/dev/null

echo "Monitoring complete. Data stored in: $MONITOR_DIR"
echo "$MONITOR_DIR" > /opt/performance-review/monitoring/LATEST
EOF

chmod +x scripts/performance_monitor.sh
Step 2: Create a combined stress workload that exercises CPU and disk I/O at the same time.

cat > scripts/generate_load.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "Generating combined CPU and I/O load for 120 seconds..."

# CPU load: two background workers computing prime factors in a loop
stress_cpu() {
    local end_time=$(( $(date +%s) + 120 ))
    while [ "$(date +%s)" -lt "$end_time" ]; do
        for i in $(seq 1 2000); do
            factor "$i" > /dev/null 2>&1
        done
    done
}
stress_cpu &
stress_cpu &

# I/O load: write and read files under /tmp
mkdir -p /tmp/io_test
(
    for i in 1 2 3; do
        dd if=/dev/zero of=/tmp/io_test/testfile_${i}.dat bs=1M count=80 2>/dev/null
    done
    sleep 30
    for i in 1 2 3; do
        dd if=/tmp/io_test/testfile_${i}.dat of=/dev/null bs=1M 2>/dev/null
    done
    rm -f /tmp/io_test/testfile_*.dat
) &

wait
echo "Load generation complete."
EOF

chmod +x scripts/generate_load.sh
Step 3: Launch the load generator in the background, then immediately run the monitor in the foreground so both cover the same 120-second window.

./scripts/generate_load.sh &
./scripts/performance_monitor.sh
