Conclusion
In this lab, students built a repeatable performance review workflow: establishing a system baseline, generating controlled CPU and disk load while collecting data from top, iostat, sar, and vmstat simultaneously, identifying bottlenecks against numeric thresholds, applying kernel-level tuning, and then re-running the identical workload to measure the effect of that tuning. The comparison report's delta column is the key artifact to interpret: a meaningfully negative delta in CPU busy or disk utilization after tuning indicates the change reduced resource consumption for the same work, while a delta near zero suggests the workload was not sensitive to the parameters adjusted, which is itself a valid and useful finding rather than a failure.

Each tuning parameter has a specific use case. Lowering vm.swappiness is appropriate on systems with sufficient RAM where you want to avoid premature swapping under memory pressure, but it can be counterproductive on memory-constrained systems where some swapping prevents out-of-memory kills. Lowering dirty_ratio and dirty_background_ratio improves I/O latency consistency by flushing writes to disk more frequently, at the cost of slightly higher overall disk write volume and reduced write-buffering benefits for bursty workloads. Switching I/O schedulers matters most on spinning disks under mixed read/write load; on modern SSDs and NVMe devices the choice of scheduler has a much smaller effect and none/mq-deadline is usually already optimal. Increasing network buffer sizes benefits high-bandwidth or high-latency network paths but wastes memory on systems with light network usage. Students should be cautious with aggressive tuning in production: values copied from a lab environment without validating against the actual workload can degrade performance, and every change made in this lab was intentionally backed up and written as a reversible sysctl file specifically so it could be rolled back.

As a next step, advanced students should extend compare_performance.sh to run multiple repetitions of each test and report a mean and standard deviation rather than a single sample, since single-run comparisons are vulnerable to noise from background processes. Students could also isolate each tunable independently, changing only swappiness in one run and only the I/O scheduler in another, to attribute performance changes to a single variable rather than a bundled set of changes.
